    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Bahá’í Faith grew from the movement of the Báb, a merchant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> who in 1844 proclaimed a new revelation, and from the mission of Bahá’u’lláh, who in 1863 declared himself the messenger of God whom the Báb had foretold.</li><li>It teaches that the founders of the great religions were messengers of one God, and that humanity must unite in a peaceful world order, with the equality of women and men, universal education and the harmony of science and religion.</li><li>Persecuted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from its beginnings to the present day, it spread from its founders’ places of exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> to almost every country, with its world centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Bahá’í calendar has nineteen months of nineteen days; during the last month, in March, Bahá’ís fast from sunrise to sunset before celebrating their New Year, Naw-Rúz, at the spring equinox.</p></aside>
    </section>

